---
import {
  ComputerTerminal01Icon,
  McpServerIcon,
  Route01Icon,
  SourceCodeIcon,
} from '@hugeicons/core-free-icons'
import CodeBlock from '@/components/CodeBlock.astro'
import FeaturePage from '@/components/FeaturePage.astro'

const related = [
  { title: 'Astro SEO', text: 'Static pages on Cloudflare, one layout for head tags, Markdown copies, sitemaps and build checks.', href: '/guides/astro-seo', icon: SourceCodeIcon, link: 'Astro SEO guide' },
  { title: 'Next.js SEO', text: 'Metadata API, prerendering, JSON-LD, sitemaps, real 404s and Markdown copies in the App Router.', href: '/guides/nextjs-seo', icon: SourceCodeIcon, link: 'Next.js SEO guide' },
  { title: 'seo crawl', text: 'Every option of the site crawl, and what each finding means.', href: '/docs/crawl', icon: Route01Icon },
  { title: 'Hosted MCP', text: 'Add the free MCP server to Claude Code, Codex, Cursor and other clients.', href: '/docs/mcp', icon: McpServerIcon },
  { title: 'CLI', text: 'Install, setup, and every command the CLI runs.', href: '/docs/cli', icon: ComputerTerminal01Icon },
]

const libraryExample = `import { auditPage, crawlSite } from 'seo'

const report = await crawlSite({ url: 'http://localhost:4321', maxPages: 500 })
console.log(report.status)
for (const group of report.issueGroups) {
  console.log(group.severity, group.title, group.count)
}

const page = await auditPage({ url: 'http://localhost:4321/about' })
console.log(page.issues.map((issue) => issue.title))`
---

<FeaturePage
  title="SEO for developers"
  description="SEO for developers: crawl local and staging builds with the open source seo CLI, give your coding agent your Search Console through MCP, and run the same checks from TypeScript."
  path="/features/seo-for-developers"
  lead="Check SEO the way you check types. Crawl the build before it ships, fail CI on real problems, and let your coding agent read your own Search Console data."
  {related}
  relatedHeading="Guides and docs"
>
  <p>
    Most SEO problems a developer causes are visible in the build: a template
    that lost its canonical, a moved page with no redirect, a staging <code>noindex</code> that reached production. SEO Skill gives you a crawler
    you can run on localhost, an MCP server for your coding agent, and a
    TypeScript library, all from the open source <code>seo</code> package.
    The commands above add the hosted MCP server or install the CLI. The
    crawl needs no sign-in, so for a one-off run, or in CI, use <code>npx -y seo</code> in place of <code>seo</code> and skip setup.
  </p>

  <h2>Crawl a local build before you deploy</h2>
  <p>
    Crawl the production build, not the dev server, so you see the HTML that
    will ship. Build with your local address as the site URL first, or every
    canonical and Markdown link will point at production and show up as an
    issue. Then start the build (<code>astro preview</code>, <code>next start</code> or <code>wrangler dev</code>) and crawl it:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock value="seo crawl http://localhost:4321 --max-pages 2000" wrap />
  <p>
    It follows links from the start page and your sitemap, and lists broken
    links, redirects, missing or duplicate titles and descriptions, canonical
    and robots tags, and structured data. It also checks what AI agents get:
    which AI crawlers your robots.txt allows, whether each page's Markdown copy
    loads, and whether <code>llms.txt</code> links work. The crawl stops at 500
    pages unless you raise <code>--max-pages</code>, and says so when it does.
  </p>
  <p>
    A <code>noindex</code> or a canonical pointing elsewhere is reported as what
    the page says. It is only a bug if you did not mean it.
  </p>

  <h2>Crawl staging and fail CI on real problems</h2>
  <p> <code>--fail-on</code> exits with an error when the crawl finds an issue at
    that severity or above, so a pipeline step can stop a deploy:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock value="npx -y seo crawl https://staging.example.com --fail-on high" wrap />
  <p>
    The crawler needs to reach staging without a sign-in. If staging sits
    behind Cloudflare Access or similar, crawl the local build in CI instead.
    For a large site, a sitemap health pass checks every listed URL's status
    and redirects without reading page bodies, and writes a JUnit file most CI
    systems can show as test results:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock value="npx -y seo crawl --sitemap-url https://staging.example.com/sitemap.xml --health --format junit --output seo-health.xml" wrap />
  <p>
    Add <code>--json --output crawl.json</code> to any crawl to keep the full
    result as a file, for a diff against the last run or for your agent to read.
  </p>

  <h2>Give your coding agent your search data</h2>
  <p>
    The crawl tells you what the site says. Search Console tells you what
    Google did with it. With the MCP server connected, your agent can answer
    questions like these from your own data while it works in the code:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Ask</th><th>What it reads</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Did last Tuesday's release change search clicks?</td><td>Clicks and positions before and after the date, with Google updates in the same window.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Is this page indexed, and which queries find it?</td><td>Google's URL Inspection result and the page's queries.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Which pages lost their clicks this month?</td><td>Pages that lost clicks, each checked live and with URL Inspection.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Which queries are we on page two for?</td><td>The striking distance section of the Search Console report.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The hosted server needs nothing installed. To run the server on your
    machine instead, with the sign-in from <code>seo start</code>, add it to
    every MCP client it finds (Claude Code, Codex, Cursor and Claude Desktop):
  </p>
  <CodeBlock value="seo mcp install --all" wrap />
  <p>
    The crawl and internal links fetch many pages, so they run only in the CLI.
    When your agent asks the hosted server for a crawl, it is told to run <code>seo crawl</code> instead.
  </p>

  <h2>Run the same checks from TypeScript</h2>
  <p>
    The package is also a library, for a build script, a test or your own
    tool. Install it in your project:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock value="npm i seo" wrap />
  <p>Then crawl a site and check a single page. Save this as <code>check.mjs</code> and run <code>node check.mjs</code>:</p>
  <CodeBlock value={libraryExample} />
  <p> <code>report.status</code> is <code>completed</code>, <code>partial</code> or <code>failed</code>. A partial crawl stopped early, for example at the
    page limit, so a missing issue in it is not an all-clear. <code>seo/mcp</code> exports the MCP server, if you want to run it inside
    your own process.
  </p>

  <h2>Set up your framework</h2>
  <p>
    The crawler finds problems; the framework guides show how to avoid them in
    the first place. The <a href="/guides/astro-seo">Astro SEO guide</a> covers
    a static site on Cloudflare with build checks that fail, and the <a href="/guides/nextjs-seo">Next.js SEO guide</a> covers the Metadata API,
    sitemaps and real 404s in the App Router.
  </p>
</FeaturePage>
